CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe autonomic overactivity secondary to misinterpreting early autoimmune pathology results. Triage appropriately mandated immediate physical evaluation to rule out an acute upper GI bleed or atypical cardiac event. Clinical examination firmly established normal hemodynamics and the complete absence of acute abdominal pathology. The rapid provision of clinical reassurance and explanation of the benign, early-stage diagnosis successfully aborted a severe panic attack, entirely preventing an unnecessary emergency department surge that could not wait until the morning.

